\newlabel{fig:app-g-ns-external-forcing}{{40}{68}{Navier--Stokes external-forcing attack perturbations across forcing families. In sample average, the gradient-generated perturbations become strongly aligned with the imposed forcing patterns, showing that the attack perturbation is not arbitrary noise but is correlated with the forcing structure of the Navier--Stokes system}{figure.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41}{\ignorespaces Navier--Stokes attack loss trace comparison. The gradient-backpropagated attack objectives and loss variants drive the attack loss upward quickly, and several methods reach much larger final losses, demonstrating that the proposed loss/backpropagation choices can produce strong loss growth during the attack.}}{68}{figure.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-ns-loss-trace}{{41}{68}{Navier--Stokes attack loss trace comparison. The gradient-backpropagated attack objectives and loss variants drive the attack loss upward quickly, and several methods reach much larger final losses, demonstrating that the proposed loss/backpropagation choices can produce strong loss growth during the attack}{figure.41}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42}{\ignorespaces Cross-benchmark attack epsilon sweep showing the best-envelope absolute loss increase for Burgers, Darcy Flow, and recurrent Navier--Stokes settings on log-log axes. The attacks are applied to trained models before adversarial training, across different adversarial-attack budgets. The approximately linear trends on the log-log plot indicate an approximate power-law relation between attack budget and loss increase. The annotations next to the curves report the fitted power laws \(y=Cx^A\).}}{69}{figure.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-cross-benchmark-epsilon-loss}{{42}{69}{Cross-benchmark attack epsilon sweep showing the best-envelope absolute loss increase for Burgers, Darcy Flow, and recurrent Navier--Stokes settings on log-log axes. The attacks are applied to trained models before adversarial training, across different adversarial-attack budgets. The approximately linear trends on the log-log plot indicate an approximate power-law relation between attack budget and loss increase. The annotations next to the curves report the fitted power laws \(y=Cx^A\)}{figure.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {43}{\ignorespaces Cross-benchmark Loss 3 optimizer ablation for Burgers, Darcy Flow, and recurrent Navier--Stokes. Burgers and recurrent Navier--Stokes use continuous \(p=q=2\) attacks. Since \(s_2(g)=g/\|g\|_2\), raw-replace and steepest-replace coincide: \(\delta _{k+1}=\varepsilon g/\|g\|_2\); add rules remain different, \(\Pi (\delta _k+\alpha g)\) versus \(\Pi (\delta _k+\alpha g/\|g\|_2)\), so NS shows three visible curves. Darcy Flow uses a binary Hamming flip budget. With \(a_i\in \{3,12\}\), the positive flip score satisfies \(\gamma _i=g_i(a_{\mathrm  {opp}}-a_i)=9|g_i|\), so raw and steepest top-\(K\) rankings coincide for both add and replace. Thus Darcy shows two visible trajectories, not missing optimizer runs.}}{69}{figure.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-cross-benchmark-optimizer-ablation}{{43}{69}{Cross-benchmark Loss 3 optimizer ablation for Burgers, Darcy Flow, and recurrent Navier--Stokes. Burgers and recurrent Navier--Stokes use continuous \(p=q=2\) attacks. Since \(s_2(g)=g/\|g\|_2\), raw-replace and steepest-replace coincide: \(\delta _{k+1}=\varepsilon g/\|g\|_2\); add rules remain different, \(\Pi (\delta _k+\alpha g)\) versus \(\Pi (\delta _k+\alpha g/\|g\|_2)\), so NS shows three visible curves. Darcy Flow uses a binary Hamming flip budget. With \(a_i\in \{3,12\}\), the positive flip score satisfies \(\gamma _i=g_i(a_{\mathrm {opp}}-a_i)=9|g_i|\), so raw and steepest top-\(K\) rankings coincide for both add and replace. Thus Darcy shows two visible trajectories, not missing optimizer runs}{figure.43}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {44}{\ignorespaces Burgers adversarial-training selected-time relative $L_2$ summary, plotted over the \(0\)--\(8\,{\rm  h}\) wall-clock window. Wall-clock time is used because different adversarial-training strategies have different per-epoch costs: solver integration provides more information but also introduces additional computation, so time-normalized comparison is the fairest efficiency measure. The random-clean variant is omitted because, in Burgers, it makes the adversarial-training loss increase rather than decrease by an extremely large amount; including it would compress and obscure the other curves, so the later Burgers training figures omit random clean as well. On the train and test curves, Loss 3 does not show a clear advantage over the other methods.}}{70}{figure.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-burgers-training-selected-time}{{44}{70}{Burgers adversarial-training selected-time relative $L_2$ summary, plotted over the \(0\)--\(8\,{\rm h}\) wall-clock window. Wall-clock time is used because different adversarial-training strategies have different per-epoch costs: solver integration provides more information but also introduces additional computation, so time-normalized comparison is the fairest efficiency measure. The random-clean variant is omitted because, in Burgers, it makes the adversarial-training loss increase rather than decrease by an extremely large amount; including it would compress and obscure the other curves, so the later Burgers training figures omit random clean as well. On the train and test curves, Loss 3 does not show a clear advantage over the other methods}{figure.44}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {45}{\ignorespaces Burgers adversarial-training generalization relative $L_2$ curves for datasets 1--25 versus wall-clock time. Loss 3 gives the clearest generalization advantage; random solver is usually second best but begins to overfit after roughly four hours. We hypothesize this occurs because random solver uses fixed, non-adaptive deltas whose statistics do not change during training, whereas Loss 3 attack deltas are regenerated against the current model--solver mismatch and can shift toward higher frequencies as the model improves.}}{71}{figure.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-burgers-training-gen-1-25}{{45}{71}{Burgers adversarial-training generalization relative $L_2$ curves for datasets 1--25 versus wall-clock time. Loss 3 gives the clearest generalization advantage; random solver is usually second best but begins to overfit after roughly four hours. We hypothesize this occurs because random solver uses fixed, non-adaptive deltas whose statistics do not change during training, whereas Loss 3 attack deltas are regenerated against the current model--solver mismatch and can shift toward higher frequencies as the model improves}{figure.45}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {46}{\ignorespaces Burgers adversarial-training generalization relative $L_2$ curves for datasets 26--50 versus wall-clock time. Loss 3 is most consistently beneficial on held-out generalization cases.}}{72}{figure.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-burgers-training-gen-26-50}{{46}{72}{Burgers adversarial-training generalization relative $L_2$ curves for datasets 26--50 versus wall-clock time. Loss 3 is most consistently beneficial on held-out generalization cases}{figure.46}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {47}{\ignorespaces Burgers Loss 3 adversarial-training attack loss and gain over 1,000 epochs, including different epsilon budget buckets. Within these buckets, the Loss 3 adversarial-attack loss increase decreases over training, indicating stronger robustness.}}{73}{figure.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-burgers-training-loss-gain}{{47}{73}{Burgers Loss 3 adversarial-training attack loss and gain over 1,000 epochs, including different epsilon budget buckets. Within these buckets, the Loss 3 adversarial-attack loss increase decreases over training, indicating stronger robustness}{figure.47}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {48}{\ignorespaces Burgers Loss 3 adversarial-training perturbation frequency content over epochs. As training progresses, Loss 3 shifts toward higher-frequency perturbations, unlike Loss 1 and Loss 2; this supports Loss 3 self-adaptivity, since a more robust model requires sharper perturbations to enlarge the model--solver error.}}{74}{figure.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:app-g-burgers-training-frequency}{{48}{74}{Burgers Loss 3 adversarial-training perturbation frequency content over epochs. As training progresses, Loss 3 shifts toward higher-frequency perturbations, unlike Loss 1 and Loss 2; this supports Loss 3 self-adaptivity, since a more robust model requires sharper perturbations to enlarge the model--solver error}{figure.48}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {49}{\ignorespaces Burgers post-training perturbation sample grid comparing the models obtained from Loss 1, Loss 2, Loss 3, and random-solver adversarial training, shown as route 1 of 6. Baseline and random clean are not plotted; the displayed models are evaluated on the same selected initial conditions. After training, all models are attacked with the same Loss 3 adversarial attack, so the labels denote the training objective rather than different evaluation objectives.}}{75}{figure.49}\protected@file@percent }
